};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/advanced/graphs-and-dynamic-programming">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Two tools unlock most of hard computer science. <strong>Graphs</strong> model anything made
    of connected things; a handful of traversal algorithms extract every classic answer (shortest
    path, cycles, ordering). <strong>Dynamic programming (DP)</strong> tames exponential blow-ups
    by remembering subproblem answers. Together they are the difference between brute force and
    elegance.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the graph–DP toolkit, in five sentences" widget="equationStep" config={graphCfg} />

  <h2>Why DP feels like magic</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The two ingredients:</strong> optimal substructure (the best answer contains best answers to smaller pieces) and overlapping subproblems (the pieces recur). Have both, and caching turns 2ⁿ into n².</li>
    <li><strong>Fibonacci is the toy:</strong> naive recursion recomputes fib(2) millions of times; memoisation collapses it to a straight line. The same move upgrades sequence alignment in biology and spell-checking in your keyboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Shortest paths at scale:</strong> Dijkstra with a priority queue is O((V+E) log V) — how Google Maps routes you around traffic in real time across millions of edges.</li>
    <li><strong>Know the boundary:</strong> some problems (travelling salesman, most scheduling) have no known polynomial algorithm — NP-hard. DP gives exact answers up to a size, then heuristics take over. Knowing when to switch is professional judgement.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Dijkstra (1959); Bellman, Dynamic Programming (1957); Kleinberg & Tardos, Algorithm Design.</p>
</TopicPage>
